<body>
<h1>Unit 22: Capacitor</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">A capacitor is an energy store defined by geometry and dielectric. The unit is where the parallel-plate formula gets derived from Gauss's law, and where students first meet the gap between an ideal component and a real one.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Capacitance is C = Q/V; for a parallel-plate capacitor C = epsilon0 epsilon_r A/d, so capacitance grows with area and shrinks with separation.</li><li>The energy stored is U = (1/2) C V^2 = Q^2/(2C), and the energy density in the dielectric is u = (1/2) epsilon E^2.</li><li>Capacitors in parallel add capacitance; in series the reciprocals add.</li><li>A capacitor stores energy rather than supplying it: an ideal charged capacitor discharges through any available path, and the discharge time constant is R C.</li><li>Real capacitors are limited by dielectric breakdown, leakage current, equivalent series resistance and self-inductance at high frequency.</li><li>Supercapacitors deliver far more power than batteries but store far less energy per kilogram, and the two technologies occupy different niches for that reason.</li><li>Capacitance depends on geometry and dielectric only, never on the charge or the voltage: adding dielectric raises the capacitance by the permittivity ratio, and a connected battery supplies the extra charge at unchanged voltage.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The parallel-plate formula assumes the separation is much smaller than the plate dimensions, so fringing can be ignored, and that the dielectric fills the gap uniformly.</li><li>In a dielectric the bound charges reduce the field by 1/epsilon_r, but the energy per unit volume usually still increases because polarisation contributes.</li><li>At DC, leakage limits how long a charge can be held; at high frequency, inductance and ESR dominate, and the ideal capacitor stops being a capacitor.</li><li>Electrolytic capacitors are polar and will be destroyed by reverse voltage, while ceramic and film types are non-polar but have different leakage and stability behaviour.</li><li>The stored energy lives in the electric field, not in the charges; discharging a capacitor does not lose that energy, it converts it back into current and heat.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Energy in 1 F at 10 V</td><td>50 J</td></tr><tr><td>Energy in a 2000 mAh cell at 3.7 V</td><td>about 26.6 kJ</td></tr><tr><td>Energy stored in a 1 F supercapacitor at 2.7 V</td><td>about 3.6 J</td></tr><tr><td>Relative permittivity of transformer oil</td><td>about 2.2</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How can more energy be stored at a higher voltage in a smaller volume?</b></p><p class="status">Engineering frontier</p><p>Solid electrolytes, nanostructured dielectrics and breakdown engineering are all being pursued, and each trades cost, reliability and lifetime against energy density.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Is there a fundamental upper bound to the energy that can be stored in a given volume?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Fundamental limits exist, including the scale at which the vacuum itself breaks down, but they lie far above present engineering practice.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Derive the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor from Gauss's law and the definition C = Q/V.</li><li>Compare the stored energy of a 1 F supercapacitor at 2.7 V with that of a 2000 mAh lithium-ion cell at 3.7 V, and compare their power delivery.</li><li>Design a smoothing network for a rectified supply and justify the ripple calculation with the RC time constant.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance">OpenStax: Capacitors and Capacitance</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-3-energy-stored-in-a-capacitor">OpenStax: Energy Stored in a Capacitor</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-4-capacitor-with-a-dielectric">OpenStax: Capacitor with a Dielectric</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
